# P084 US customs duty payments and the duty-mitigation services market: 8 ideas (round 2)

Written 2026-10-18. About 15 WebSearch calls, 8 fallback-script searches and 9 page fetches (over the brief's 8-15 because several first answers were thin). Pool P084 is the industrial slice, pool 8: about $214B of US customs duties in the twelve months to July 2026, with the services fee pool a small fraction.

Evidence labels: [primary] I opened the document itself; [secondary] law-firm alert, trade press, vendor page or search summary; [memory] my background knowledge, not re-verified; [derivation] my arithmetic, shown; [assumption] a number I chose and name as such.

## Pool facts used (checked this run unless marked)

- Duties: $214B trailing twelve months to July 2026 [secondary, tracker from the pool file]. 2025 imports for consumption: $3,391.2B [primary, Census related-party release of 2026-07-07]. Collected duty rate is therefore about 6.3% of imports [derivation]. The NY Fed put the weighted statutory rate at 2.6% at the start of 2025 and 13% by December 2025 [secondary].
- Related-party imports 2025: $1,663.1B, 49.0% of imports. This covers US firms' foreign subsidiaries and US subsidiaries of foreign parents [primary, Census].
- Entries: CBP processed about 2.7-2.9M entry summaries a month in early 2025 [secondary], so roughly 33-35M a year [derivation].
- Customs brokerage: about $5.48B US market in 2026 (Mordor, paid estimate) [secondary]; 14,454 active licensed brokers (CBP FAQ) [secondary]; exam pass rate about 15% [secondary].
- CBP HQ H350722, dated 2026-01-16 [primary, PDF read]. An unlicensed platform doing OCR extraction of entry data, giving AI classification beyond six digits tied to a prospective entry, filing CBP Form 5106, or passing powers of attorney through itself is conducting customs business. A six-digit general research database with a disclaimer is permitted (HQ H272798).
- 17 CFR 210.2-01(c)(5) [primary]: an accountant is not independent if it provides a product or service to an audit client for a contingent fee or commission.
- 19 USC 1313 [primary]: drawback is 99% of duties; entry within 5 years of importation; under (j)(2) the claimant must be the importer or have received same-HTS8 merchandise directly or indirectly from the importer; transfers can be shown with ordinary business records; the importer stays liable for a claimant's claim up to the lesser of duties claimed or authorized, (k)(2).
- Section 232 restructure, effective 2026-04-06: duty on full customs value of metal derivatives, Annex I-A 50%, I-B 25%, Annex III transitional (total 15%) through 2027-12-31; goods in the annexes with under 15% aggregate metal weight are not subject, except Chapters 72, 73, 74, 76 (CBP CSMS 68253075); articles made entirely with US-melted or US-smelted metal pay 10%; Russian-smelted aluminum 200% [secondary, Thompson Hine, BHFS, GHY].
- Section 301 forced-labor tariffs on 60 economies, effective 2026-07-24: 10% for 17 economies including Canada, Mexico, India, UK; 12.5% for the rest including China, Vietnam, EU; USMCA-qualifying goods exempt. Master case Burlap and Barrel v. US plus a 25-state suit; hearing 2026-09-30, no ruling found [secondary]. No exclusion petition process found; Section 232 exclusions closed since 2025-02-10 [secondary].
- DOJ Trade Fraud Task Force (August 2025) passed $1B in recoveries on 2026-07-14; Perfectus Aluminum $549.5M FCA settlement 2026-05-12 (qui tam); Ceratizit $54.4M for Section 301 origin misdeclaration, December 2025 [secondary].
- IEEPA: struck down 2026-02-20; about $170B of duties [pool digest]; CBP's CAPE refund portal had accepted declarations for 25.1M entries by 2026-07-31 [secondary]. CBP built a free pipe for importer refunds within months.

---

## 1. Intercompany Reconciler (lens: insider, a customs-valuation partner / trade counsel with 20 years across a Big Four firm and a trade boutique)

- **One-liner:** Software plus a licensed filer that sets, documents and reconciles the intercompany price on every related-party import, so customs duty, income tax and the year-end transfer-pricing true-up stop contradicting each other.
- **Customer:** US subsidiaries and parents of multinational groups with more than $250M a year of related-party imports. Half of all US imports (49.0%, $1.66T) are related-party.
- **Payer and signer:** Group head of tax or VP Tax and Trade, with the US entity CFO. Budget lines: tax and transfer-pricing advisory, and the customs duty line itself. The fee is a share of verified duty saved, so no new budget line is needed.
- **Problem:** For a related-party import the customs value is usually the intercompany price, accepted if the circumstances of sale or test values support it (19 USC 1401a) [secondary]. The tax team sets that price under IRC 482 and trues it up after year end. Those true-ups almost never reach customs. A downward adjustment is a lost refund unless entries were flagged for CBP Reconciliation (reconcile within 21 months, with a formula that existed in advance) or fixed by post-summary correction (about 300 days) or protest (180 days after liquidation) [secondary]. An upward adjustment is unpaid duty and penalty exposure (one customs firm quotes 200-800% of lost revenue) [secondary]. Section 1059A links the two: it caps US tax basis at customs value, though IRS PLR 202552012 (December 2025) eased that in a deductive-value case and binds only the requester [secondary]. At a 13% statutory rate versus 2.6% a year earlier, every 1% of customs value is worth about five times what it was.
- **Solution:** The platform ingests the ERP intercompany price file, the transfer-pricing model and ACE entry data (through a power of attorney to a licensed broker partner, later its own licence). It computes SKU-family price corridors that sit inside both the tax arm's-length range and the customs test values, writes the formula documentation before goods enter, sets Reconciliation flags at entry, tracks the 21-month deadline, files reconciliations, corrections and protests, and matches the tax team's year-end true-ups to entries. It also produces the circumstances-of-sale binder CBP asks for. For parents in countries taxed above the US, a lower import price also cuts global tax.
- **Why now:** Tariff rates are five times higher. Groups are renegotiating who bears the tariff, and the instrument is the transfer price. The December 2025 PLR shows the tax side is moving. DOJ's trade-fraud push (Perfectus $549.5M) makes sloppy under-declaration costly, so the upward direction needs control too.
- **Wedge:** US distribution subsidiaries of Japanese, Korean and German industrial and auto-parts groups with $200M-$2B of related-party imports and limited-risk-distributor models, where the US margin is fixed and tariff cost flows straight into the price corridor. First 10: a free diagnostic on 12 months of ACE entry data plus the intercompany price file, sold through mid-tier trade counsel and transfer-pricing boutiques.
- **Path to $100M:** Related-party imports $1,663B times the 6.3% collected rate is about $105B of duty on those goods [derivation; assumes related-party goods pay the average rate]. Target 300 groups averaging $1.2B of related-party imports, $360B, which is 22% of the pool. A defensible 2% customs-value reduction at a 7% blended duty saves $504M a year; at a 20% fee that is $101M. At a 1% reduction it takes 600 groups. This needs more than 20% of related-party value or about half of the top 600 groups, so it is a heroic share. The group-concentration assumption (a few hundred to 1,500 groups hold most related-party imports) is unsourced [assumption]; verify with Census LFTTD or CBP ACE data.
- **Who does it today:** Big Four customs and transfer-pricing practices (EY, KPMG, PwC, Deloitte all publish on it), Forvis Mazars, EisnerAmper, trade counsel; generic transfer-pricing software (Supernomial t0, Thomson Reuters ONESOURCE) with no customs link; customs software (SAP GTS, Descartes, Thomson Reuters) with no transfer-pricing link. I searched for a vendor that reconciles the two and found none.
- **Why they lose:** The Big Four split tax and customs into separate practices, bill hourly, and cannot take contingent fees from audit clients. Transfer-pricing tools have no ACE connection. Customs tools never see the ERP price file. Brokers file entries but never see the group's pricing policy. Nobody owns the 21-month reconciliation calendar.
- **Moat (7 Powers):** Switching costs. The product sits inside the ERP intercompany price file, the year-end close and ACE reconciliation flags that run 21 months. Counter-positioning against hourly advisers is secondary.
- **Earned secret:** Customs and tax each treat the intercompany price as the other team's input. CBP recognises a downward adjustment only if entries were flagged for Reconciliation, or fixed inside a correction window, under a formula that existed beforehand. Almost nobody writes the formula before the goods enter. For parents in countries taxed above the US (Japan, Germany; [memory]), a lower import price is rarely a trade-off, because it cuts duty and global tax together.
- **First 90 days:** Hire two former customs-valuation partners and engage a licensed broker as responsible supervisor. Get a written opinion from trade counsel on circumstances of sale, test values and Section 1059A. Run free diagnostics on five US subsidiaries to find true-ups that never reached customs, in either direction. Sign three on a capped savings share. Build the flag-and-formula pack for Japan-parented distributors first.
- **Outside-the-box score:** 8/10.

## 2. Rights Ledger (lens: insider, a drawback specialist with 20 years at a broker and a boutique)

- **One-liner:** A standard rights notice and shared ledger that lets a distributor that paid duty on imported goods pass the drawback right down the sales chain to the customer who exports the same goods, with insurance for the importer's liability.
- **Customer:** US manufacturers and exporters that buy imported inputs from US distributors and importers (the claimants), and those distributors, who hold the entries.
- **Payer and signer:** The claimant. Controller or VP Tax of the exporting manufacturer pays a contingent 15% of the refund. The distributor gets a revenue share and the indemnity; its CFO signs.
- **Problem:** Under 19 USC 1313(j)(2) the claimant need only have received same-HTS8 goods, directly or indirectly, from the importer, and ordinary invoices prove the transfer [primary]. But the exporter does not know which entries a supplier several hops upstream paid duty on. The importer is liable for the claimant up to the lesser of duties claimed or authorized, (k)(2), and gets nothing, so every distributor refuses. The right goes unclaimed. Paid drawback ran about $0.9-1B a year (GAO average $896M for 2011-18) [secondary]; the vendor claims that 80% is unclaimed are marketing and I do not rely on them.
- **Solution:** A machine-readable rights notice generated from the importer's ERP and attached to each B2B sale (HTS8, quantity, entry number, duty paid, MFN or 301 flag). The claimant side aggregates notices, matches its exports at HTS8 and files through a licensed drawback broker partner. The platform supplies standard contracts and an insurer-backed indemnity for the (k)(2) liability, and pays the importer a share.
- **Why now:** The July 2026 Section 301 duties reach inputs from the EU, Japan, India and the rest of 60 economies, far beyond China, and vendor summaries say they are drawback-eligible after a CBP Chapter 99 fix in August (verify the CSMS) [secondary]. The 5-year lookback makes 2021-26 imports claimable. IEEPA duties are out (they go through CAPE) and Section 232 is mostly barred, so the base is MFN plus 301. CBP now runs ACE refunds at volume.
- **Wedge:** MRO, fastener, bearing, chemical and electrical distributors that import over $20M and sell to export-heavy OEMs. First 10: three distributors plus seven of their exporter customers, sold bottom-up through exporter controllers.
- **Path to $100M:** US goods exports were $2,179.9B in 2025 (domestic $1,759.1B) [primary]. Assume manufactured domestic exports of $1.2T, imported-input content of 12% ($144B) and a blended MFN-plus-301 rate of 6%: about $8.6B a year of eligible duty [assumption chain]. Against about $1B actually paid, that is a large gap. $100M at a 15% fee is $667M of claims filed a year, about 8% of the eligible base or roughly 10% of the unclaimed part. Verify the base by matching Census export HTS8 and import HTS8 per firm.
- **Who does it today:** Drawback specialists (Sandler & Travis, Livingston, UPS, Kuehne+Nagel; [memory]); vendors Pax, Consyn, Lightsource, GingerControl, Passport (e-commerce), Tariff Refund HQ, Zollback; Flexport tariff-refund automation; CBP's own free ACE drawback module. All serve one claimant company at a time. I found no cross-company rights ledger and no drawback marketplace.
- **Why they lose:** A single-claimant vendor cannot get the importer (the supplier's supplier) to cooperate. The importer's broker is paid by the importer and gains nothing. The importer carries the (k)(2) risk. So vendors claim only on the client's own imports.
- **Moat (7 Powers):** Network economies. Each distributor on the ledger makes every exporter's claim cheaper, and each exporter makes joining worth more to the distributor. Counter-positioning against per-client vendors.
- **Earned secret:** The drawback right travels with the goods, not with the importer. A manufacturer can claim on duty a distributor three hops upstream paid, matching only at the 8-digit HTS, and ordinary invoices prove the chain. What kills claims is the distributor's fear of the importer-liability clause, not the law.
- **First 90 days:** Write the rights-notice schema. Get an insurer quote for the (k)(2) indemnity. Recruit five distributors and replay 2021-26 ACE data against ten exporters' export files to show dollars. Hire a licensed drawback broker. File one pilot claim.
- **Caveats:** GAO has reported CBP not manually checking some drawback claims, which led to incorrect refunds [secondary], so tighter CBP scrutiny and fraud controls are real risks. Section 232 and IEEPA duties are not claimable.
- **Outside-the-box score:** 6/10.

## 3. Origin Rail (lens: pattern, liability-shifting mandate becomes a per-event verification rail, plus flip which side pays)

- **One-liner:** A certified third-party attestation per shipment of where the goods were last substantially transformed, where the metal was smelted and what the metal weight fraction is, paid per shipment by the importer and by a yearly verified profile that the foreign factory buys.
- **Customer:** Importers of industrial goods from Asia, the EU and Mexico whose duty depends on origin and composition, and the foreign factories that supply them.
- **Payer and signer:** The importer's VP Trade Compliance or General Counsel pays $15-30 per shipment from the legal-risk and compliance budget. The factory's export sales director pays $1.5-3k a year to keep the US customer.
- **Problem:** Origin now sets the rate (10% versus 12.5% under the new 301, legacy China 301 on top, Russian-smelted aluminum at 200%). The importer carries the proof. A CBP request for information or an EAPA case gives about 30 days to produce factory records, and the Task Force is converting origin errors into False Claims Act cases (Ceratizit $54.4M, Perfectus $549.5M). The weak link is a foreign factory that does not answer email.
- **Solution:** A rail anchored by a licensed broker. Factories onboard once (documents, a partner audit or video plus scale and XRF checks). Each shipment carries a signed statement that the entry data references. Exports go out as a CBP-format evidence binder under a counsel-directed structure (privilege matters, because a vault of bad facts is discoverable). Usage is billed by API call.
- **Why now:** The FCA wave, the Section 232 full-value cliff, country-specific 301 rates, and H350722, which stops unlicensed vendors from preparing entry data and so pushes anchoring onto a licensed entity.
- **Wedge:** Importers of aluminum and steel derivatives from Vietnam, Malaysia, Taiwan and Thailand. First 10: mid-size HVAC, electrical and machinery component importers plus each one's top 20 suppliers.
- **Path to $100M:** 25,000 verified factories at $2,000 is $50M, plus 2.5M attested shipments at $20 is $50M. 2.5M shipments is about 7% of roughly 35M entries [derivation], or about 25% of an assumed 10M evidence-sensitive entries [assumption]. The number of foreign factories shipping to the US is unverified (CBP manufacturer-ID counts not found). $20 is 0.2% of the duty on a $50,000 shipment at 25%. The age-check analogue, Yoti, earns about 10 cents a check, so the per-check price here must carry real duty-at-stake [digest].
- **Who does it today:** Altana (Product Passports; about $322M raised; CBP two-year contract signed October 2025; bought customs-automation startup Cervo AI on 2026-07-24) [secondary]; Sayari, Sourcemap, Everstream, Resilinc, Interos (supply-chain mapping and risk; [memory]); SGS, Intertek, Bureau Veritas (factory audits; [memory]); Oritain and Applied DNA (forensic origin; [memory]).
- **Why they lose:** Altana sells to CBP, and its WTO case study describes importers opting in to share compliance data with CBP, so importers hesitate to give it adverse evidence. Mapping vendors sell dashboards, not signed per-shipment attestations. Audit firms visit factories, not shipments, taking weeks at $1-5k a visit. None anchors a licensed filer.
- **Moat (7 Powers):** Network economies, because a factory is verified once and every US customer reuses it. Cornered resource: the verified factory file.
- **Earned secret:** Importers lose CBP evidence requests because a factory in another time zone does not answer within 30 days, not because the law is against them. Factories answer when an order depends on it. So the party the factory must please (its US customer) should require the rail, and the factory should pay for it.
- **First 90 days:** Sign three importers that have already had a CBP request for information. Onboard 30 of their suppliers and assemble one full evidence binder. Settle the privilege structure with FCA counsel. Define the attestation schema around CF-28 and Section 232 Annex IV. Choose a licensed-broker anchor.
- **Outside-the-box score:** 5/10.

## 4. Licence Bank (lens: pattern, buy the services firm and rebuild its margin, plus credentialed people sold to a new concentrated buyer class)

- **One-liner:** Buy licensed customs brokerages for their licences, licensed supervisors and importer powers of attorney, run them on one AI filing stack priced per clean entry with a correction warranty, and rent the licensed chassis to the software vendors that H350722 now locks out.
- **Customer:** Mid-market importers that use three to five small brokers, and the AI and software vendors (Tarifflo-type auditors, classification tools, marketplaces) that need a licensed back end.
- **Payer and signer:** Importers' logistics director or CFO, from the existing brokerage-fee line (about $5.48B a year in the US). Vendors' COO, from cost of revenue.
- **Problem:** The only legal way to run AI over entries is inside a licensed broker. H350722 treats unlicensed OCR extraction, 10-digit AI classification tied to a prospective entry, Form 5106 filing and power-of-attorney pass-through as customs business [primary]. Licensed supply is capped: 14,454 active licence holders and a pass rate near 15% [secondary]. Most brokerages are small and owner-run, and entries got harder (stacked Chapter 99 lines).
- **Solution:** Acquire 30-60 owner-operated brokerages with earn-outs. Move them onto one stack where licensed brokers supervise AI-prepared entries. Price per clean entry, with a warranty covering corrections and penalties up to a cap (outcome pricing). Sell the licensed chassis to vendors at a wholesale per-entry fee.
- **Why now:** H350722 (2026-01-16). AI-native entrants are unlicensed or small: Alchemize (YC S26), Amari ($4.5M seed, February 2026, sells to over 30 brokerages), Altana with Cervo AI (July 2026). Flexport's standalone brokerage grew 86% in a year [secondary]. Owners are ageing.
- **Wedge:** Five brokerages in one port cluster (Houston, Los Angeles) bought from retiring owners; their importers are the first customers.
- **Path to $100M:** $100M is 1.8% of a $5.48B pool [derivation]. That is about 35 firms at $2.9M revenue or about 110 at the $0.88M average ($5.48B over about 6,200 firms; the firm count is unverified). I found no price data, so I assume 0.5-1.0x revenue, or $50-100M of capital [assumption]. The revenue is bought, so it is not comparable to organic ARR.
- **Who does it today:** Flexport Customs LLC (national permit); Livingston (Platinum Equity, 2019); Expeditors, UPS, DHL, Kuehne+Nagel, DSV; Southfield-backed Alba Wheels Up (bought Steer and others); about 6,200 small firms [unverified]; tool vendors Amari, Digicust, Nabu, Alchemize.
- **Why they lose:** Forwarders bundle customs with freight at low prices and cannot sell outcome pricing [inference]. PE roll-ups bought brokers without rebuilding the stack. Tool vendors that sell to brokers do not own the customer. Flexport competes with every forwarder it would serve.
- **Moat (7 Powers):** Cornered resource (licences, licensed supervisors, powers of attorney) plus scale economies in cost per entry.
- **Earned secret:** The licence is the cap. A licensed individual must exercise responsible supervision and control, so entries filed are limited by licensed headcount, not by clerks [my reading of 19 CFR Part 111]. AI removes the clerk limit and leaves the licence as the scarce asset.
- **First 90 days:** Pull the CBP broker list. Shortlist 40 firms with owners over 60. Sign letters of intent with three. Hire a chief licensed broker. Choose build versus buy for the filing engine. Line up seed capital and an acquisition facility. Sign two vendors for the chassis.
- **Caveat:** This sits on the cliché list as an AI roll-up. It survives only because the licence is scarce and H350722 is new.
- **Outside-the-box score:** 3/10.

## 5. Second Filer (lens: dilemma, the independent contingent auditor that the Big Four, law firms, brokers and software cannot be)

- **One-liner:** An independent, licensed post-entry auditor that works only for a share of recovered duty and delivers CBP-ready workpapers, aimed at large importers whose Big Four auditors are barred from contingent fees.
- **Customer:** About 2,000 large US importers: public companies and PE-backed groups with $20M or more of annual duty.
- **Payer and signer:** VP Tax or Director of Global Trade; the CFO approves the savings share. No budget line is needed because the fee comes out of recoveries.
- **Problem:** CBP liquidates almost every entry as entered without checking whether the importer overpaid [memory]. Recoveries need post-summary corrections (about 300 days), protests (180 days after liquidation), drawback, FTA claims, valuation fixes (freight, assists, royalties) and classification corrections. Tarifflo reports that 1 in 8 of more than 1M entries it audited was overpaid (self-reported) [secondary]. The Big Four are barred from contingent fees on audit clients (17 CFR 210.2-01(c)(5)) [primary]. Law firms bill hourly and cannot share fees with non-lawyers [memory]. Brokers cannot audit their own filings. Unlicensed software cannot file corrections (H350722).
- **Solution:** A licensed broker entity working under counsel. Ingest five years of ACE entry-summary line data. AI plus human review of classification, valuation, origin, FTA eligibility, 301 exclusions and MPF caps. File corrections, protests and drawback through the licensed entity. Underpayments found go to counsel under privilege for the prior-disclosure decision. Fee is 25-30% of cash recovered, plus a prevention subscription.
- **Why now:** Duty is six times higher. DOJ's task force and the qui tam surge (Perfectus) make underpayment findings urgent. Tarifflo has shown demand at the small end. Big Four customs teams are swamped.
- **Wedge:** Big-Four-audited mid-cap manufacturers and distributors with $20-150M of annual duty. First 10: introductions from PE operating partners.
- **Path to $100M:** The top 1% of trading firms hold about 80% of trade value (Bernard, Jensen, Redding, Schott; [memory]), so about 2,000 importers hold about $170B of duty [assumption]. Target 600 at an average of $80M: $48B. Recoveries of 0.6% (12.5% of entries overpaid times about 5% of entry duty; from Tarifflo's self-reported rate) are $288M. At a 30% fee that is $86M, plus $30M from 300 prevention subscriptions at $100k, about $116M. That needs 30% of the top 2,000, a heroic share, and the 0.6% recovery rate is unverified.
- **Who does it today:** Big Four customs practices (EY, KPMG, PwC, Deloitte); Sandler & Travis, Livingston; Tarifflo (YC S26, about $520k raised, small and mid-size importers, broker arrangement unclear); GingerControl; Pax; Flexport tariff-refund automation; brokers' own correction services.
- **Why they lose:** Big Four: barred from the contingent model on their audit clients, and hourly. Law firms: hourly and cannot scale. Brokers: audit would expose their own errors and the E&O claim. Software: not licensed. A new entrant that is licensed, independent, contingent and counsel-supervised is the only one with no conflict.
- **Moat (7 Powers):** Counter-positioning, plus process power from audit playbooks and cross-client benchmarks.
- **Earned secret:** CBP liquidates by operation of law, as entered, so nobody inside government checks for overpayment. The only quality control on duty paid is whoever audits. For audit-client importers, the firm that knows the files best is legally barred from sharing in the recovery.
- **First 90 days:** Stand up the licensed entity and a counsel arrangement. Run three free sample audits on mid-cap importers. Write the privilege protocol for underpayment findings. Measure recovered dollars per dollar of duty. Sign the first three contingent contracts. Map PSC and protest limitation periods.
- **Outside-the-box score:** 5/10.

## 6. Peer Ledger (lens: dilemma, a neutral importer-owned data pool that Altana, manifest-data vendors, brokers and the Big Four cannot be)

- **One-liner:** An importer-controlled, firewalled pool of how real importers classify, value and document the same parts, giving each member an outlier score before CBP or a whistleblower finds it, and giving sureties, lenders, insurers and big buyers a compliance-risk score on consent.
- **Customer:** Importers with $250k or more of annual duty, and the institutions that carry importer risk.
- **Payer and signer:** Importers: the director of trade compliance or GC, $3-10k a year. Risk buyers: head of surety underwriting, an asset-based lender's credit officer, a PE operating partner, $100-200k a year for a data feed.
- **Problem:** Classification is judged on consistency and documentation (reasonable care). Each broker sees only its own clients, CBP sees everything, and importers see nothing. Public manifest data (Panjiva, ImportGenius) shows what shipped, not what was declared. Supply-chain graph vendors sell to CBP, so importers do not feed them adverse facts. Sureties, lenders and PE buyers now carry six times the duty exposure and price it blind.
- **Solution:** With the importer's consent, pull its entry-summary line data through ACE Reports and a power of attorney to a licensed broker partner. Encrypt it into a pool governed by the members. Score each part's classification, valuation and origin against peer ranges, and keep a reasonable-care file. Release scores to third parties only on the member's consent.
- **Why now:** FY2025 CBP audits were revised up to 465 completed [secondary]; FCA cases are surging; CAPE proved ACE data can be pulled in volume; error cost has risen fivefold; H350722 pushes any data vendor toward a licensed anchor.
- **Wedge:** One trade association (HVAC, hardware, furniture components). First 10: 10 importers pull their ACE reports and see their outliers on a million lines.
- **Path to $100M:** Assume 40,000 importers with $250k or more of duty [assumption]. 11,000 at $5k is $55M, plus 300 institutional feeds at $150k is $45M, total $100M. That is 27% of importers, a heroic share. Counts are unsourced; CBP importer-of-record and duty-band counts would settle it.
- **Who does it today:** Altana (supply-chain graph; Product Passports; CBP contract; estimated $37.5M ARR for 2024 per Latka, which is unreliable); S&P Panjiva, ImportGenius, Descartes Datamyne (manifest data); Thomson Reuters ONESOURCE (rules); Zonos Classify; Tarifflo; Flexport.
- **Why they lose:** Altana cannot promise a CBP-blind vault. Manifest vendors do not hold declarations. Brokers each hold one slice and owe client confidentiality. The Big Four are siloed by client.
- **Moat (7 Powers):** Network economies, and a cornered resource in consented declared-data.
- **Earned secret:** Classification is not decided by a rule; it is defended by consistency and records, and the only party that sees every declaration for a part is CBP. A pool lets importers see their own file the way CBP does. [I found no primary source describing CBP's peer-outlier targeting; this part is inference.]
- **First 90 days:** Sponsor with one trade association. Pull ACE reports from ten importers. Build an outlier score on a million lines. Get an antitrust and confidentiality opinion. Pitch two customs sureties.
- **Outside-the-box score:** 6/10.

## 7. Weight Line (lens: shock, Section 232 full-value duty with a 15% metal-weight cliff)

- **One-liner:** A bill-of-materials-to-weight engine and evidence file that tells importers of Section 232 derivatives which SKUs sit within reach of the 15% metal-weight line (below it the duty is zero, above it 25-50% of full value) or the 10% US-metal lane, and does the redesign, supplier change and documentation to cross it.
- **Customer:** Importers of annex-listed derivative goods: machinery, electrical equipment, appliances, fittings, vehicle parts.
- **Payer and signer:** VP Supply Chain with VP Engineering; COO or CFO signs. Budget is the tariff line in cost of goods; the fee is a share of avoided duty.
- **Problem:** Since 2026-04-06 duty falls on the full customs value of annex goods, but goods outside Chapters 72-76 with under 15% metal by aggregate weight are exempt (CSMS 68253075) [secondary]. Articles made entirely with US-melted or US-smelted metal pay 10%. A two-point change in weight fraction can move 25-50 points of price. Yet bill-of-materials weights sit in design engineers' files and factory records. Brokers never ask for them and engineers do not know the tariff. Russian-smelted aluminum content draws 200%.
- **Solution:** PLM and ERP connectors; supplier BOM weight collection with spot-check scales or XRF at partner labs; weight fractions by SKU; a heat map of distance to the line; redesign options (material substitution, separate entry of the metal component, US assembly where lawful); an evidence file; a mill-certificate chain for the US-metal lane; refreshed as inclusions change. Entries go through a licensed partner.
- **Why now:** The 2026-04-02 proclamation, the Annex IV technical corrections of 2026-05-06, the new rolling inclusion authority, and DOJ trade-fraud enforcement that punishes wrong weights.
- **Wedge:** HVAC, electrical gear and small-machinery makers and importers with $20-200M of annex imports from Chinese, Vietnamese and Mexican factories. First 10: referrals from brokers who see the new duty on every entry.
- **Path to $100M:** Assume $150B of annex-listed imports at a 25% blended rate, so $37.5B of duty [assumption; the one-day check is to pull Census import value for the annex HTS lines]. A 10% fee on $1B of avoided duty is $100M, which is 2.7% of that base. Equivalent: $60k software across 600 importers is $36M, plus $64M of success fees.
- **Who does it today:** Trade counsel and consultants (tariff-engineering advice); SGS, Intertek and Bureau Veritas (composition testing; [memory]); PLM vendors Siemens, PTC, Dassault (no tariff module; [memory]); supply-chain mapping vendors Sourcemap and Altana; classification tools from Thomson Reuters, Descartes and TariffLens AI. I searched for a tool that computes distance to the weight line and found none.
- **Why they lose:** Brokers and classification tools stop at the HTS number. PLM vendors do not know the tariff. Testing firms sell one-off tests per sample. Consultants bill hourly. Each side lacks the other side's data.
- **Moat (7 Powers):** Process power and switching costs (BOM integration, evidence files); cornered resource in supplier weight data.
- **Earned secret:** The 15% line is a weight fraction on the aggregate of the applicable metals, and the proof is records, not a lab test. The people who hold the records have never been asked by a broker. Tariff engineering is lawful, and here two points of weight can buy 25-50 points of price.
- **First 90 days:** Build the weight-fraction calculator on five real BOMs. Pull Census import value for the annex lines to size the pool. Pilot with three importers. Get a counsel memo on the documentation standard and on how CBP verifies. Sign a lab partner.
- **Risks:** CBP may require certificates or change the threshold or lists; verification practice is unknown; Section 232 is statutory and has been durable, unlike IEEPA.
- **Outside-the-box score:** 8/10.

## 8. Pass-Through (lens: shock, the IEEPA ruling creates a new buyer: downstream customers who paid the surcharge)

- **One-liner:** A buyer-side recovery-audit firm for tariff surcharges that finds every explicit surcharge on a customer's supplier invoices, matches it to the supplier's refund and contract clause, recovers the buyer's share of IEEPA refunds on contingency, then audits every future Section 232 and 301 surcharge against the duty actually owed.
- **Customer:** B2B buyers (contractors, OEMs, distributors, retailers) that paid separately listed tariff surcharges to suppliers.
- **Payer and signer:** Controller or CFO, with the chief procurement officer. A contingent 20-25% of recovered dollars; no budget line.
- **Problem:** IEEPA duties (about $170B) were struck down on 2026-02-20 and refunds go only to the importer of record (CAPE had accepted 25.1M entries by 2026-07-31). Customers that paid itemised surcharges (for example "a temporary tariff surcharge of 12%") have contract and equity claims. Law-firm alerts (Jones Walker, February 2026; Carter Ledyard, July 2026; Legal500, August 2026) say B2B buyers with explicit surcharges have stronger claims than consumers, and tell them to send demands [secondary]. Importers that hold refunds book them: Fastenal's Q3-2026 gross margin cites "tariff refunds" [primary, 8-K via the pool file]. Suppliers will not volunteer, and accounts payable cannot parse the claims. Going forward, 232 and 301 surcharges are unverifiable and change monthly.
- **Solution:** Ingest accounts-payable and invoice data and detect surcharge lines. A clause engine reads supplier terms. Send demands with refund-evidence requests supplier by supplier, negotiate, and take recoveries as credits. Then run a standing audit of surcharges against actual rate stacks, annex treatment and exemptions, and pass through future refunds (Section 122 and 301, if struck) the same way. Legal advice stays with a partner law firm.
- **Why now:** Refunds are flowing now, claims doctrine is forming, and the buyer's position is strongest while the supplier still holds the cash. Pending cases on Section 122 and the new 301 could repeat the pattern.
- **Wedge:** Mid-size contractors and industrial buyers with $50M or more a year of purchases from distributors that added surcharges (construction, HVAC, electrical). First 10: design partners found through contractor associations.
- **Path to $100M:** Take $170B of IEEPA duty [digest]. If 10% reached B2B customers as explicit surcharges ($17B; range 5-20%; unsourced) and 35% is recovered ($6B), a 22% fee pool is about $1.3B, so $100M is about 8% of it. The standing audit of ongoing surcharges is smaller (about $15B a year times 5% overbilling times 25% is about $190M), so $100M there would need about half. This is a spike of two to three years unless refund events recur. Be clear about that.
- **Who does it today:** Law firms (Jones Walker, Carter Ledyard & Milburn, Arnall Golden Gregory); One Eagle Advisory (sells tariff-refund recovery for downstream buyers); consumer class-action firms; accounts-payable recovery auditors (PRGX; [memory]); Big Four.
- **Why they lose:** Law firms bill hourly and cannot process thousands of supplier relationships. Recovery auditors lack ACE and tariff-stack knowledge. The distributors that hold refunds cannot volunteer them without giving up margin.
- **Moat (7 Powers):** Weak. Process power from clause and settlement data; counter-positioning. I would not call this defensible beyond the window.
- **Earned secret:** The refund goes to one legal name on the entry, but the loss sat on invoice lines in other companies' payables. The invoice with surcharge text is the evidence, and only the buyer holds it.
- **First 90 days:** Sign five buyers. Pull 24 months of accounts-payable data for surcharge text. Build clause matching. Send demands to each buyer's top 20 suppliers through a partner law firm. Track response and settlement rates. Confirm that non-lawyer demand letters stay on the right side of the unauthorized-practice line.
- **Outside-the-box score:** 5/10.

---

## Killed or set aside (so nobody re-spends time)

- **Duty financing** (Slope, Flexport Capital, HSBC TradePay for Import Duties already sell it; credit-heavy; a five-minute idea).
- **Drawback rights exchange:** 19 USC 1313 ties the right to the merchandise chain, so a free-floating market is not lawful. The Rights Ledger is what survives.
- **Exclusion-request factory:** no Section 232 exclusions since 2025-02-10 and no petition path found for the new 301.
- **Section 122 / 301 protest factory:** the IEEPA precedent is a free CBP pipe (CAPE) within months.
- **Customs bond MGA:** premium pool about $150M at full share (bond principal about 10% of $214B times about 0.7%; [derivation with premium rate from memory]).
- **Customs due diligence for PE:** about $170M at 100% share.
- **Customs penalty insurance:** fines and duties are not fortuitous losses.
- **First-sale-native sourcing house:** market size unverified.
- **USMCA qualification desk:** overlaps pool 10 and Origin Rail; USMCA review outcome unverified.

## Open checks (cheap, would change the scores)

1. Census import value for the Section 232 annex HTS lines (sizes Weight Line).
2. Census LFTTD or CBP ACE counts of importers by duty band and group concentration (sizes Reconciler, Second Filer, Peer Ledger).
3. CBP CSMS confirmation that the 2026 Section 301 Chapter 99 codes are drawback-eligible (Rights Ledger).
4. Brokerage acquisition prices (Licence Bank).
5. Share of B2B tariff surcharges that were itemised and carried refund-sharing clauses (Pass-Through).
